.\" UNIX V7 source code: see /COPYRIGHT or www.tuhs.org for details.
.\" REWRITTEN rather than corrected, per cmd/README.md SS10's `touch' rule: v7's DESCRIPTION
.\" said "interpret words" and the word it meant -- the PDP-11's 16 bits -- has stopped
.\" existing.  See cmd/od/README.md.
.TH OD 1
.SH NAME
od \- octal dump
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B od
[
.B \-bcdhowx
] [ file ] [ [
.B +
]offset[
.BR ". " "]["
\fBb\fR ] ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Od
dumps
.I file
in
one or more formats
as
selected by the first argument.
If the first argument is missing,
.B \-o
is default.
.PP
A
.I word
here is the machine's own: 48 bits, six bytes.
The word formats
.RB ( \-o ,
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-x )
show one, and the byte formats
.RB ( \-b ,
.BR \-c )
show its six bytes.
An output line is
.B twelve bytes
\- two words.
.PP
The meanings of the format argument characters
are:
.TP 3
.B b
Interpret bytes in octal, three digits each.
.TP 3
.B c
Interpret bytes as characters.
Certain non-graphic characters appear as C escapes:
null=\e0,
backspace=\eb,
formfeed=\ef,
newline=\en,
return=\er,
tab=\et;
others, including every byte above 0177, appear as 3-digit octal numbers.
.TP 3
.B d
Interpret words in decimal, 15 digits.
.TP 3
.B o
Interpret words in octal, 16 digits.
.TP 3
.B w
The same as
.BR \-o ,
under the name that says what it dumps.
.TP 3
.B x
Interpret words in hex, 12 digits.
.B \-h
is a second name for it.
.PP
The
.I file
argument specifies which file is to be dumped.
If no file argument is specified,
the standard input is used.
.PP
The offset argument specifies the offset
in the file where dumping is to commence.
This argument is normally interpreted
as octal bytes.
If `\fB.\fR' is appended, the offset is interpreted in
decimal.
If `\fBb\fR' is appended, the offset is interpreted in
blocks of 3072 bytes.
If the file argument is omitted,
the offset argument must be preceded
.RB ` + '.
.I Note:
the offset argument also sets the radix of the
.B address
column, as a side effect v7's page never mentioned \-
.B x
or
.B 0x
makes it hexadecimal, a leading
.B 0
octal, and a `\fB.\fR' anywhere decimal.
.PP
A line identical to the one before it is not printed; a
.B *
stands for any run of them.
Dumping continues until end-of-file, and the byte offset of the end is printed last.
.SH "THE WORD IS 48 BITS"
.I Note:
.BR \-o ,
.B \-d
and
.B \-x
mean what they meant on the PDP\-11 \-
.I the machine word, in this radix
\- so nothing about them has been redefined.
What changed is the machine word, from 16 bits to 48, and with it the width of the column:
.PP
.nf
      flag        v7              here
      \-o \-w       6 octal         16 octal
      \-d          5 decimal       15 decimal
      \-x \-h       4 hex           12 hex
.fi
.PP
The six bytes of a word are packed
.B most significant first,
which is how six characters occupy a word on this machine and what
.IR getw (3)
and
.IR putw (3)
already do.
So
.B od \-c
and
.B od \-w
of the same file agree about which byte is which, and a file written by
.I putw
reads back as its own words.
A short group at the end of a file is padded with zero bytes on the right, which is
where the unused characters of a partial word are.
.SH "A BLOCK IS 3072 BYTES"
.I Note:
the
.B b
suffix on an offset counts filesystem blocks, which are 3072 bytes here,
where v7 counted 512-byte PDP\-11 disk blocks.
This agrees with
.IR tail (1)'s
.B \-b
and with
.IR dd (1)'s
.B b
suffix.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
b6disasm(1), getw(3)
